From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: irisrun <irisrun@localhost>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 16:28:49 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 14/37] Build, stroke and trim paths in Swift

Path's move(to:), addLine(to:), curves, arcs, rectangles, ellipses and
rounded rectangles were unimplemented on every platform, and so were
strokedPath and forEach on Linux: any Path built by hand or shape with
.stroke() crashed. OpenRenderBox cannot hold paths on Linux yet, so paths
keep their elements in Swift, in a new Storage case. Arcs follow CoreGraphics,
whose clockwise is in y-up coordinates, as cubic curves of a quarter turn at
most.

strokedPath keeps the line and the stroke style, and trimmedPath the line and
the fractions, so a renderer can stroke the line itself. Hit-testing fills by
winding number, or for a stroked path, by distance to the line; curves are
flattened into 16 segments for that, for bounds and for trimming.
